# {{ ansible_managed }}
# =============================================================================
# smartd — monitor the drives, notify through nothing
# =============================================================================
# THE ABSENCE OF `-m` IS THE DECISION, not an oversight. Do not add it back
# without reading this.
#
# The stock line was `-m root -M exec /usr/share/smartmontools/smartd-runner`,
# and it had been mailing root since installation. Measured on 2026-08-29:
# postfix runs on both hosts with `inet_interfaces = loopback-only` and an EMPTY
# `relayhost`, so the mail could never leave the machine — 10 deliveries in seven
# days, 9 of them smartd's, all of them into /var/mail/root where nobody reads.
#
# --- Why the answer is silence and not a better channel ----------------------
# The first instinct was to push these alerts into Uptime Kuma. Then the mails
# were read, and every one of them was REDUNDANT:
#
#   8x  "Currently unreadable (pending) sectors"  -> attribute 197, which
#       homelab-disk.sh already reads with a more careful rule than smartd's:
#       it alarms when the count RISES, when there is no completed self-test to
#       weigh it against, or when the last completed self-test was not clean.
#       That rule worked. The monitor went DOWN from 2026-08-23 to 08-26 on a
#       real read failure at LBA 730424728 and cleared when a new extended test
#       completed without error.
#
#   2x  "Self-Test Log error count increased"     -> the self-test log, which
#       homelab-disk.sh parses on two axes (the newest entry, and the newest
#       COMPLETED entry) precisely so an abort is not read as a failure.
#
# So the daily report covers this ground, and covers it better. A second channel
# would have added a notification path, a monitor to create by hand and a
# false-positive budget, to say the same things a day sooner about a class of
# event — slow surface degradation — that does not turn on a day.
#
# smartd still runs and still logs to the journal. What it no longer does is
# write to a mailbox that cannot forward.
#
# `-n standby` keeps it from spinning an idle drive up just to ask its
# temperature; `-d removable` keeps a disconnected USB enclosure from being
# treated as a vanished device.
DEVICESCAN -d removable -n standby
